        <div class="article-body">
          <p>Recently, Qwen3.5 has been one of the hottest models in the community. It’s a vision-language model that’s relatively small and performs well in coding benchmarks. That means I can host it locally on my M-series Mac and use it with <code>opencode</code>.</p>
          <p>I didn’t find many resources online about setting it up, so here’s a short guide.</p>

          <ArticleCallout
            labelId="prerequisite-title"
            label="Prerequisite"
          >
            Qwen3.5 now includes 0.8B, 2B, 4B, and 9B models, so you can run it on any M-series Mac. The 4B model needs about 3.4 GB of RAM; 9B needs about 6.6 GB. For the larger models, 27B needs 17 GB, which is tight on a 24 GB machine.
          </ArticleCallout>

          <h2>Choosing a model</h2>
          <p>Qwen3.5 comes in different architectures, parameter sizes, and quantization levels. The 27B model is dense; the 35B model is a mixture-of-experts model. The latter runs faster, while parameter count and quantization affect memory use.</p>

