    <template id="long-form-content">
      <header class="article-header">
        <p class="eyebrow">Field guide <span aria-hidden="true">/</span> Debugging</p>
        <h1>Debugging HTTP traffic with tcpdump and tshark</h1>
        <p class="dek">Capture HTTP traffic, inspect packet data, and find the useful details in a PCAP file.</p>
        <div class="metadata">
          <time datetime="2026-03-22">March 22, 2026</time>
          <span class="tag">tcpdump</span>
          <span class="tag">tshark</span>
        </div>
      </header>
      <div class="article-body">
        <p>There are times when you need to debug an issue in your HTTP service and realize that your logs don't have enough information. One way to troubleshoot is to capture network traffic and peek into request and response data.</p>
        <p>Sounds hard? It isn't. You can do that with <code>tcpdump</code>, then analyze it with Wireshark or its command-line tool, <code>tshark</code>.</p>
        <p>Here's a quick write-up on capturing HTTP traffic with <code>tcpdump</code> and filtering it with <code>tshark</code>. You can jump to the <a href="#cheat-sheet">cheat sheet</a> for a summary.</p>

        <ArticleQuote
          parts={[
            {
              kind: "text",
              text: "If your services use HTTPS, the packets are encrypted and won't be readable without credentials.",
            },
          ]}
        />

        <h2>Prerequisites</h2>
        <p>If you want to follow along, here are the tools we need:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><code>tcpdump</code></li>
          <li><code>tshark</code></li>
          <li><code>jq</code></li>
          <li><code>python3</code> or anything that can spin up a web server</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Here's a minimal Python server with a JSON endpoint, which we can use to generate a response:</p>
        <ArticleCodeBlock
          ariaLabel="Python HTTP server example"
          language="python"
          highlightedHtml={pythonServerCodeHtml}
        />
        <p>Save this as <code>server.py</code>, then run it with <code>python3 server.py</code>. Use <code>curl localhost:8080/books</code> to generate a JSON response.</p>

        <h2>Capturing traffic</h2>
        <ArticleCallout
          labelId="capture-warning"
          label="Disclaimer"
        >
          Captured traffic can contain credentials, cookies, and personal information. Get approval before capturing traffic in a production environment.
        </ArticleCallout>
        <p>Capturing traffic with <code>tcpdump</code> is straightforward. Add <code>-w</code> to save it to a file for later analysis:</p>
        <ArticleCodeBlock
          ariaLabel="Commands for capturing HTTP traffic"
          language="bash"
          highlightedHtml={tcpdumpCodeHtml}
        />
        <p>Use <code>curl localhost:8080/books</code> to generate a request. Press <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>C</kbd> to stop the capture and see its summary.</p>

        <h2>Filtering and formatting</h2>
        <p>After installing <code>tshark</code>, use it to inspect a capture, filter for HTTP packets, or print selected fields:</p>
        <ArticleCodeBlock
          ariaLabel="Commands for inspecting a PCAP file"
          language="bash"
          highlightedHtml={tsharkCodeHtml}
        />

        <h2 id="cheat-sheet">Cheat sheet</h2>
        <p>Here are a few of the commands covered in the article:</p>
        <ArticleTable
          ariaLabel="HTTP traffic debugging commands"
          caption="Common commands for capturing and reading HTTP traffic."
          columns={["Goal", "Command"]}
          rows={[
            {
              heading: { kind: "text", text: "Capture traffic" },
              cells: [{ kind: "code", text: "sudo tcpdump -i any port 8080" }],
            },
            {
              heading: { kind: "text", text: "Save a capture" },
              cells: [{ kind: "code", text: "sudo tcpdump -i any port 8080 -w output.pcap" }],
            },
            {
              heading: { kind: "text", text: "Generate a request" },
              cells: [{ kind: "code", text: "curl localhost:8080/books" }],
            },
            {
              heading: { kind: "text", text: "Read a capture" },
              cells: [{ kind: "code", text: "tshark -r output.pcap" }],
            },
            {
              heading: { kind: "text", text: "Show HTTP packets" },
              cells: [{ kind: "code", text: "tshark -r output.pcap -Y 'http'" }],
            },
          ]}
        />
        <p class="table-note">Adapted from “Debugging HTTP traffic with tcpdump and tshark.”</p>
        <p>These tools can feel intimidating at first, but the basics are approachable once you know where to start.</p>
      </div>
