    <template id="fujifilm-content">
      <header class="article-header">
        <p class="eyebrow">Field notes <span aria-hidden="true">/</span> Photography</p>
        <h1>Converting Fujifilm RAW files to JPEG with CLI</h1>
        <p class="dek">Extract the JPEG already embedded in Fujifilm RAW files, then batch-convert a folder with a small Python script.</p>
        <div class="metadata">
          <time datetime="2026-01-22">January 22, 2026</time>
          <span class="tag">Fujifilm</span>
          <span class="tag">CLI</span>
        </div>
      </header>
      <div class="article-body">
        <p>I have been shooting with a Fujifilm camera for a while. There are times when I need to quickly convert my RAW files to JPEG.</p>
        <p>In macOS, you can quickly convert a RAW file to JPEG through <code>Right Click → Quick Actions → Convert Image</code>.</p>
        <ArticleFigure
          src={quickActionImage.src}
          width={quickActionImage.width}
          height={quickActionImage.height}
          alt="The macOS Quick Actions menu with Convert Image selected."
          caption="Convert a RAW image from Finder’s Quick Actions menu."
          presentation="integrated"
        />
        <p>You can also select all the RAW files and convert them to JPEG in bulk. It takes about 10 seconds to convert 17 files and place them in the same folder. Not too bad.</p>
        <p>While this approach works, it isn't efficient for a large number of RAW files. I also want the JPEG files placed in a separate folder, so I decided to write a small CLI to do this more efficiently.</p>

        <ArticleCallout labelId="fujifilm-skip-label" label="Lazy to read?">
          Skip to <a href="#converting-all-raw-files-in-a-folder">the folder conversion script</a> to convert every Fujifilm RAW file to JPEG and place the files in a separate folder.
        </ArticleCallout>

        <h2>Converting Fujifilm RAW files to JPEG programmatically</h2>
        <p>The first step is to figure out how to convert Fujifilm RAW files to JPEG programmatically.</p>
        <p>It turns out that in macOS, we can use <code>sips</code> to convert a RAW file to JPEG:</p>
        <ArticleCodeBlock ariaLabel="Convert a Fujifilm RAW image with sips" language="bash" highlightedHtml={fujifilmSipsCommandHtml} />
        <p><code>sips</code> stands for scriptable image processing system. It comes with macOS by default.</p>
        <ArticleCodeBlock ariaLabel="The sips command description" language="text" highlightedHtml={fujifilmSipsInfoHtml} />
        <p>I was introduced to <code>sips</code> while vibe coding a CLI to convert RAW files in bulk. It has been available in macOS for a long time.<sup><a href="#fujifilm-note-1" aria-label="Footnote 1">1</a></sup></p>
        <p>Good enough? Not quite. It takes roughly 500 ms to convert one RAW file to JPEG with <code>sips</code>:</p>
        <ArticleCodeBlock ariaLabel="sips conversion timing" language="text" highlightedHtml={fujifilmSipsTimingHtml} />
        <p>I often have hundreds of RAW files to convert. Even using all 14 cores in parallel, it would still take about 18 seconds.<sup><a href="#fujifilm-note-2" aria-label="Footnote 2">2</a></sup></p>

        <h2>A faster way</h2>
        <p>It turns out that Fujifilm RAW files already contain JPEG data.<sup><a href="#fujifilm-note-3" aria-label="Footnote 3">3</a></sup></p>
        <ArticleCodeBlock ariaLabel="Fujifilm RAW file structure diagram" language="text" highlightedHtml={fujifilmRafLayoutHtml} />
        <p>By summing the header bytes, we can determine that the JPEG offset starts at byte 84:</p>
        <ArticleCodeBlock ariaLabel="Calculation of the JPEG offset" language="text" highlightedHtml={fujifilmOffsetMathHtml} />
        <p>Bytes 84–87 store the JPEG offset, while bytes 88–91 store the JPEG length. Armed with those two values, we can extract the JPEG data:</p>
        <ArticleCodeBlock ariaLabel="Pseudocode for extracting the embedded JPEG" language="text" highlightedHtml={fujifilmPseudocodeHtml} />
        <p>The next block shows the first 127 bytes of a Fujifilm RAW file:</p>
        <ArticleCodeBlock ariaLabel="Hex dump of the Fujifilm RAW header" language="text" highlightedHtml={fujifilmHexylOutputHtml} />
        <p>We can use <code>hexdump</code> to inspect the eight bytes that contain the offset and length:</p>
        <ArticleCodeBlock ariaLabel="Hex dump of the JPEG offset and length" language="text" highlightedHtml={fujifilmHexdumpOutputHtml} />
        <p>Those values are hexadecimal. The offset bytes, <code>00 00 00 94</code>, equal 148; the length bytes, <code>00 4a 52 31</code>, equal 4,870,705:</p>
        <ArticleCodeBlock ariaLabel="Decimal values of the JPEG offset and length" language="text" highlightedHtml={fujifilmByteValuesHtml} />
        <p>Because the length is the number of JPEG bytes, it also tells us that the image is about 4.9 MB.<sup><a href="#fujifilm-note-4" aria-label="Footnote 4">4</a></sup></p>

        <h2>Extracting JPEG bytes with CLI</h2>
        <p>From here, there are multiple ways to extract the bytes.</p>

        <h3>Using <code>dd</code></h3>
        <p>One option is to use <code>dd</code>:</p>
        <ArticleCodeBlock ariaLabel="Extract JPEG bytes with dd" language="bash" highlightedHtml={fujifilmDdCommandHtml} />
        <p>This takes 11 seconds, much slower than <code>sips</code>, because it reads and writes one byte at a time:</p>
        <ArticleCodeBlock ariaLabel="dd extraction timing" language="text" highlightedHtml={fujifilmDdOutputHtml} />

        <h3>Using <code>tail</code> and <code>head</code></h3>
        <p>Another alternative is to use <code>tail</code> and <code>head</code> together:</p>
        <ArticleCodeBlock ariaLabel="Extract JPEG bytes with tail and head" language="bash" highlightedHtml={fujifilmTailCommandHtml} />
        <p>It takes roughly 300 ms, slightly faster than <code>sips</code>:</p>
        <ArticleCodeBlock ariaLabel="tail and head extraction timing" language="text" highlightedHtml={fujifilmTailTimingHtml} />

        <h3>Using your own code</h3>
        <p>We can also write a small program to extract the JPEG. Here is a short Python script:</p>
        <ArticleCodeBlock ariaLabel="Python code to extract one JPEG from a RAF file" language="python" highlightedHtml={fujifilmSmallPythonHtml} />
        <p>This takes about 30 ms, ten times faster than the <code>tail</code> and <code>head</code> approach.</p>
        <ArticleCodeBlock ariaLabel="Python extraction timing" language="text" highlightedHtml={fujifilmPythonTimingHtml} />

        <h2>Combining everything into a single script</h2>
        <p>You could write a Bash script to glue everything above together, or use Python to do it all.<sup><a href="#fujifilm-note-5" aria-label="Footnote 5">5</a></sup></p>
        <ArticleCodeBlock ariaLabel="Python script to extract a JPEG from one RAF file" language="python" highlightedHtml={fujifilmExtractScriptHtml} />
        <p>Save the code as <code>extract_jpeg.sh</code>, then make it executable:</p>
        <ArticleCodeBlock ariaLabel="Make the extraction script executable" language="bash" highlightedHtml={fujifilmMakeExecutableHtml} />
        <p>Then run it with a RAW file:</p>
        <ArticleCodeBlock ariaLabel="Run the single-file extraction script" language="text" highlightedHtml={fujifilmSingleFileOutputHtml} />
        <p>The script takes about 33 ms to complete:</p>
        <ArticleCodeBlock ariaLabel="Single-file script timing" language="text" highlightedHtml={fujifilmSingleFileTimingHtml} />

        <h2 id="converting-all-raw-files-in-a-folder">Converting all RAW files in a folder</h2>
        <p>With that building block, we can extend the script to accept a folder path and convert every RAW file into a JPEG in a separate output folder. Here is the Python script:</p>
        <ArticleCodeBlock ariaLabel="Python script to convert a folder of Fujifilm RAW files" language="python" highlightedHtml={fujifilmFolderScriptHtml} />
        <p>It takes about 50 ms to convert 17 RAW files to JPEG:</p>
        <ArticleCodeBlock ariaLabel="Batch conversion output for 17 RAW files" language="text" highlightedHtml={fujifilmFolder17OutputHtml} />
        <p>Most of the overhead comes from starting Python:</p>
        <ArticleCodeBlock ariaLabel="Python startup timing" language="text" highlightedHtml={fujifilmPythonStartupHtml} />

        <h2>Conclusion</h2>
        <p>I also tried it with a folder containing 630 RAW files. It now takes about 2.4 seconds:</p>
        <ArticleCodeBlock ariaLabel="Batch conversion output for 630 RAW files" language="text" highlightedHtml={fujifilmFolder630OutputHtml} />
        <p>Could it be faster? Definitely. But this should be sufficient for now.</p>
        <p>That's it. Hope you learned something from this post.</p>

        <section class="article-references" aria-label="Footnotes">
          <ol>
            <li id="fujifilm-note-1"><span class="article-footnote-number" aria-hidden="true">1</span><span>Based on <a href="https://til.simonwillison.net/macos/sips">this article</a>, which refers to older documentation.</span></li>
            <li id="fujifilm-note-2"><span class="article-footnote-number" aria-hidden="true">2</span><span>500 ÷ 14 ≈ 36 batches × 500 ms ≈ 18 seconds.</span></li>
            <li id="fujifilm-note-3"><span class="article-footnote-number" aria-hidden="true">3</span><span>The <a href="https://libopenraw.freedesktop.org/formats/raf/">RAF file format documentation</a>.</span></li>
            <li id="fujifilm-note-4"><span class="article-footnote-number" aria-hidden="true">4</span><span>4,870,805 bytes is about 4.9 MB.</span></li>
            <li id="fujifilm-note-5"><span class="article-footnote-number" aria-hidden="true">5</span><span>The Python script was written by AI (Claude Haiku 3.5).</span></li>
          </ol>
        </section>
      </div>
    </template>

